    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 5px;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Study Habits for Success</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> How I Learned to Focus</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Evaluate arguments in opinion pieces. Reading: Make basic inferences or predictions about a text content from headings, titles, or headlines.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can evaluate and reflect on arguments in study habits articles and make connections to personal academic experiences (e.g., "The article suggests using checklists. I've tried this strategy, and it has helped me stay organized."). Can make inferences about the main focus of a section based on its title and supporting text (e.g., "The section titled 'Boost Your Focus' likely discusses concentration techniques such as minimizing distractions and using a timer during study sessions."). Can predict the type of advice provided in a section on time management by examining headings and context clues (e.g., "The heading 'Tips for Daily Progress' suggests that the section will include steps for creating a daily routine and using calendars or to-do lists.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f0f8ff;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of key concepts.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a series of images showing different study environments and habits (e.g., organized desk, messy desk, someone focused, someone distracted by phone). Students quickly scan the images and raise their hand if they can identify one *effective* or one distracting *habit*. Teacher asks: "What *do you observe* about these *study habits*?" and "What *used to be* your biggest *challenge* when trying to *focus*?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher presents a short blog post title, "5 Ways I *Have Developed* Better *Focus*," and its main headings (e.g., "Setting Clear *Goals*," "Managing Your *Time*," "Minimizing *Distractions*"). The teacher models how to *predict* the content of each section just from the heading, e.g., "From 'Setting Clear *Goals*', I *can infer* this section *might discuss* how to *prioritize assignments*." Teacher models pronunciation of /sk/ in "skill" and /st/ in "study," emphasizing the difference.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a heading is 'Managing Your Time,' *what kind of advice should* you expect to read about?" (Focus on *management, strategies, routines*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does it mean to 'evaluate arguments' in an article?" (Elicit "to decide if the ideas are *effective* or *logical*").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to the Scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher provides students with an opinion piece titled "My Journey to Better *Focus*: How I Overcame Procrastination" (approx. 250-300 words, B1.1 level). The article is divided into sections with clear headings (e.g., "Recognizing the Challenge," "Building New *Routines*," "Celebrating Small *Achievements*"). The text integrates target vocabulary (*procrastinate, develop, improve, manage, focus, set goals, organize, effective, consistent, productivity, success*) and grammar (present perfect for achievements, past simple for reflection).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Opinion piece/blog post excerpt, printed or projected.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpret and critically analyze factual and opinion-based texts on familiar topics, identifying key information and evaluating arguments.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> Students read the title and headings only and answer: "What is the main topic of this article?" and "What do you *predict* the author *might discuss* in the section 'Building New *Routines*?'"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students quickly read the article to determine if 3-4 statements related to the author's general experience are True or False. (e.g., "The author *used to procrastinate* on *assignments*," "The author *has achieved* better *grades* since changing *habits*.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match each section heading to a short summary (1-2 sentences) of its main idea. This directly addresses making inferences about main focus from titles and supporting text.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading, targeting specific language features.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading for specific details, evaluating arguments, making inferences. Grammar: Present perfect for achievements, Past simple for reflecting. Vocabulary: *effective, prioritize, organize, manage, develop, reflect, strategy, improvement, success, focus, progress, achieve, consistent*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Gap-fill/Vocabulary in Context):</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with sentences extracted from the article, containing blanks where target vocabulary words (*strategies, productivity, improvement, management, results, effort*) or past simple/present perfect verbs (*developed, used to, has improved*) have been removed. Students fill in the blanks, choosing from a word bank. This reinforces reading comprehension at a lexical and grammatical level.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Argument Evaluation & Prediction):</b> In pairs, students re-read sections of the article. For each section, they identify an argument or piece of advice. Then, they *evaluate* it using modal verbs and adverbs, e.g., "The author suggests using *checklists*. I *think this might be* a very *effective strategy* for *time management* because I've seen good *results*." Or, based on a heading, "The heading 'Boosting Your *Productivity*' *suggests* the author *could talk about* using *timers* during *study* sessions." Teacher encourages practice of -ed endings like /d/ in "improved" and /t/ in "accomplished."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (WH-questions & Reflection):</b> Students ask each other WH-questions about the article and their own experiences. E.g., "What *have you done* to *improve* your *focus*?" "How *did you manage* your *assignments* last week?" "What *used to be* your biggest *distraction*?" They reflect on the author's arguments and compare them to their own personal academic experiences, reinforcing pragmatic functions of expressing achievements and past habits.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small group discussion and presentation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher provides each group with a different short (100-150 words) opinion piece excerpt or a new section from the original article, each focusing on a specific *study strategy* for "Study Habits for Success" (e.g., "The Pomodoro Technique for *Focus*," "Mindfulness for *Learning*," "The Power of *Organized* Notes"). Each excerpt has a title and a key argument. Students in groups will:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Read and Evaluate:</b> Read their assigned excerpt and *evaluate* the argument presented. They *should discuss* its *potential effectiveness* and connect it to their own *study experiences*. (e.g., "This article *suggests* short breaks are good. I *have tried* this, and it *has improved* my *productivity*.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Make Inferences/Predictions:</b> Based on the title and content of their excerpt, they *predict* what kind of advice another section of the original article (e.g., one titled "Overcoming *Procrastination*") *might offer*, connecting it to the theme "How I Learned to Focus." (e.g., "The section 'Overcoming *Procrastination* ' *could suggest* setting *small, achievable goals* to *manage* time *effectively*.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Present:</b> Each group briefly presents their evaluation of the strategy and their prediction for another section to the class. They *should use* target vocabulary like *manage, improve, develop, reflect, suggest, set (goals), practice, achieve, organize, accomplish, effective, consistent* and grammar like present perfect ("We *have discussed*...") and past simple ("We *used to think*..."). They also practice stress on main verbs like "have *developed*."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Sociolinguistic application:</b> Groups *negotiate plans* and *share experiences* about *study strategies*, discussing cultural and social norms (e.g., "Let's create a *study schedule* where we review one subject per day and take 10-minute breaks after 40 minutes of studying. We *should use* the Pomodoro technique, where we study for 25 minutes, then take a 5-minute break. This *helps us stay focused* without feeling *overwhelmed*.").</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment and student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and peer assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students’ group presentations from Stage 4. Each group member also completes a brief written reflection.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist & Peer Feedback):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Evaluation:</b> Were arguments from the text clearly identified and *evaluated* (e.g., "The article *suggests* X. We *think this is effective* because...")?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Accuracy of Predictions/Inferences:</b> Were predictions about other sections logical and supported by textual cues? (e.g., "Based on the heading, we *predict* the text *could discuss*...").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Language:</b> Did students use vocabulary related to *study habits, focus, strategies, improvement*, and grammar (present perfect for achievements, past simple for reflection, modals for predictions/suggestions)?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Focus on clarity of speech, pronunciation of specific sounds (/s/, /st/, /sk/, -ed endings), and stress on main verbs (e.g., *have developed*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> How well did the group *manage* to *discuss* and *prioritize* ideas?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What *have you learned* today about *evaluating* reading materials or *making predictions*?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which *study strategy* mentioned today *do you think might be most effective* for your *own learning*, and why?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection for continuous improvement.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How has reading and *evaluating* articles about *study habits helped* you *reflect* on your *own routines*?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What *new strategies* or *ideas have you gained* from the readings and discussions today that *you might try*?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students *evaluate arguments* and *make inferences*? (e.g., "The matching activity was very *effective* for identifying main ideas.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments are needed for future reading lessons or scaffolds for students who struggled? (e.g., "Some students found *evaluating arguments* challenging; *I should provide* more sentence starters next time.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can the learned vocabulary and reading strategies be integrated into the next lesson?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction):</b> Students will use the *evaluated study strategies* and their *personal reflections* to *discuss and share* their *experiences* and *future plans* for *improving their focus* in pair and group speaking activities. The vocabulary related to *habits, focus, strategies, achievements*, and *time management* will be recycled and actively produced in spoken interactions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>